content.text:
I read the `durable-objects` skill and its rules reference before reviewing. None of the Cloudflare API tools apply to a code review, so this is a code review only.

## Problems

1. **`setTimeout` can't be relied on for a 1-hour delay.** The runtime evicts a Durable Object soon after it stops receiving requests. When that happens the in-memory timer is gone and `flush()` never runs. Each request also starts its own timer, so if the object stays alive, N requests produce N flushes.
2. **`alarm()` never runs.** Nothing calls `ctx.storage.setAlarm()`, so the alarm handler is dead code.
3. **Flushed data is never removed.** `pending` is never deleted after a successful send, so every flush posts the same items again.
4. **HTTP errors look like success.** `fetch` only rejects on network errors, not on 4xx/5xx responses. A 500 from the sink lets `alarm()` return normally, so the runtime doesn't retry it. Alarms are retried with backoff only when the handler throws.
5. **Empty batches get sent.** When `pending` is undefined, the body is the string `"null"`.
6. **Items added during a flush can be lost.** Other requests can run while `await fetch(...)` is outstanding. If you fix problem 3 by deleting the whole `pending` key, anything appended during the POST is deleted without being sent. Remove only the items you actually sent.
7. **The sink can receive a batch twice.** Alarms run at least once, so a batch can be redelivered if the object crashes after the POST but before the storage update. Send a batch id the sink can use to drop duplicates.
8. **Nothing in this class writes `pending`.** If another method appends to it, that method should also schedule the alarm.

## Suggested version

```ts
import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers";

const FLUSH_DELAY_MS = 60 * 60 * 1000;

export class Room extends DurableObject {
  async fetch(request: Request) {
    const count = ((await this.ctx.storage.get<number>("count")) ?? 0) + 1;
    await this.ctx.storage.put("count", count);
    await this.scheduleFlush();
    return new Response(String(count));
  }

  // Call this wherever items are appended to "pending".
  private async scheduleFlush() {
    if ((await this.ctx.storage.getAlarm()) === null) {
      await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + FLUSH_DELAY_MS);
    }
  }

  async alarm() {
    const pending = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    if (pending.length === 0) return;

    const batchId = crypto.randomUUID();
    const res = await fetch("https://example.com/sink", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "content-type": "application/json", "idempotency-key": batchId },
      body: JSON.stringify(pending),
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`sink returned ${res.status}`); // triggers alarm retry

    // Drop only what was sent. Items appended during the POST are kept.
    const current = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    const remaining = current.slice(pending.length);
    if (remaining.length) {
      await this.ctx.storage.put("pending", remaining);
      await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + FLUSH_DELAY_MS);
    } else {
      await this.ctx.storage.delete("pending");
    }
  }
}
```

The `slice` works only if `pending` is append-only. If other code edits it in other ways, store each item under its own key or in a SQLite table and delete by id. For a new class, SQLite storage (`new_sqlite_classes`) and RPC methods in place of `fetch()` are the recommended setup.

I read the Durable Objects skill and found eight problems: the in-memory timer is lost when the object is evicted, the alarm is never scheduled, and the flush resends data, ignores HTTP errors and can lose items. The replacement above schedules a single storage alarm, throws on a failed send so the runtime retries, removes only the items it sent, and adds an idempotency key so the sink can drop repeated batches.
